      "dom_id": "question-53009",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223346/assets/1b0dd854ae2edb38245466049806c7e93bb4b704a03284905d968869fa1b4146.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the kite above?\n\nI. $AB=AD.$\n\nII. The kite has a pair of congruent opposite angles.\n\nIII. $AB=BC=CD=AD.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. In the figure, we can see that $AB=AD.$\n\n- Statement II is true. A kite always has a pair of congruent opposite angles.\n\n- Statement III is false. A kite is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. However, not all sides are congruent, for instance, $AB\\ne BC.$\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true.",
